    "section_evoo_v3_faq_rRJhLR": {
      "type": "section-evoo-v3-faq",
      "blocks": {
        "faq_item_wg78Aq": {
          "type": "faq_item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is 2mg really enough? Most brands sell 5-10mg.",
            "answer": "<p>2mg is not a compromise. It is the evidence-appropriate dose. Your body's own peak nocturnal melatonin is 0.1-0.3mg. Clinical research on melatonin supplementation consistently shows that 1-3mg is effective for sleep onset support. Doses above this do not improve sleep onset times they increase the risk of morning grogginess, daytime drowsiness, and residual sedation. The 5-10mg products are not superior. They are oversupplemented. AIWO Melatonin SR 2mg gives you the biological signal your brain actually needs  at the dose physiology uses.<br/><br/></p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_item_BFc6Am": {
          "type": "faq_item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What does \"Sustained Release\" mean and why does it matter?",
            "answer": "<p>Immediate-release melatonin dissolves fast, peaks fast, and clears fast. It supports sleep onset but provides little coverage for the second half of your sleep window. If you are waking at 3am or 4am and struggling to return to sleep, your immediate-release supplement has already been metabolised. Sustained release means the tablet uses a controlled matrix that releases melatonin gradually across the night supporting both sleep initiation and sleep maintenance. This more closely mirrors the natural nocturnal melatonin curve your pineal gland produces in response to darkness.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_item_pgG9dR": {
          "type": "faq_item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Will my body stop making its own melatonin if I supplement?",
            "answer": "<p>At 2mg, there is no established evidence of suppression of endogenous melatonin production. The pineal gland responds to light cues not to supplemental melatonin dose. This is meaningfully different from hormone replacement, where exogenous supplementation directly suppresses endogenous production. Consult your healthcare professional for guidance on long-term use.<br/><br/></p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_item_zet6LP": {
          "type": "faq_item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I use this for jet lag?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. Melatonin's direct action on the suprachiasmatic nucleus your brain's master circadian clock makes it the most studied intervention for circadian phase resetting after trans meridian travel. Take one tablet before boarding a long flight or before local bedtime at your destination, as advised by your healthcare professional.<br/><br/></p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_item_Q6TUxh": {
          "type": "faq_item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How long before I notice a difference?",
            "answer": "<p>Sleep onset improvements may be noticed within the first week of consistent use. Sleep maintenance improvements typically build over 2-4 weeks as your circadian rhythm stabilises around the SR signal. For objective measurement, track with AIWO 181 sleep-related biomarkers at baseline and 60 days. Individual results vary. Consult your healthcare professional if sleep concerns persist.<br/><br/></p>"
          }
        }
      },
